# yew performance suite

This directory contains yew's performance contracts, measurements, and
committed runner baselines. `budgets.txt` is the single machine-readable
source for limits. A baseline records observed performance; it never changes
a budget.

## Start with yew's profiler

For any interactive latency symptom, the first move is to restart with
`YEW_PROF=1`, reproduce it, and run `:prof`. Read the phase table before
reaching for `perf`: the built-in profiler answers *which phase* in one
keystroke, with a measured overhead limit of 2 percent. External profilers
answer narrower questions and must never supply a gate number.

Useful commands:

```text
:prof                  report p50/p90/p99, shares, and worst frames
:prof frames           show the last 40 raw frames
:prof frames 200       show the last 200 raw frames
:prof mark paste-100k  label the next frame before reproducing a spike
:prof reset            discard old samples without disabling profiling
:prof dump /tmp/yew-prof.txt
```

All five commands refuse with `profiling is off; restart with YEW_PROF=1`
when profiling is disabled. For headless work, use
`YEW_PROF=1 YEW_PROF_OUT=/tmp/yew-prof.txt build/yew --batch SCRIPT.fl ...`.
Batch frames are top-level script statements and intentionally contain only
the dispatch, jobs, and syntax phases.

`TOTAL` describes every event-loop wake, including background and timer
frames. `KEYPAINT` uses the same measured total but only for frames with one
decoded key and nonzero terminal output. The external keypress-to-paint
cross-check uses `KEYPAINT`, and hard-fails if its call count differs from
the harness's painted-key count; comparing external samples with all wakes
would mix two different populations.

## Triage playbook

| Symptom | Likely phase | First look | Then |
|---|---|---|---|
| Typing lags only in one file | `YEW_PH_SYN` | `:prof` share | `yew syn check --coverage`; inspect the per-line syntax p99 |
| Every file lags, worse on large files | `YEW_PH_DISPATCH` | `:prof` p99 and worst frames | callgrind for an accidental O(n) line walk |
| Lag appears only with LSP or AI | `YEW_PH_JOBS` | `:prof frames` around the spike | inspect generation counters and stale-response rejection |
| Lag appears after a long session | all phases or `unaccounted` | RSS checkpoints | Sprint 57 allocation accounting; check cache growth/fragmentation |
| Frame count exceeds key count | dispatch/event loop | deterministic frames-per-key row | restore drain-then-render ordering |
| `unaccounted` is high | uninstrumented work | add the missing phase boundary | `perf record -g` to identify the owning function |
| `poll` is nonzero inside a burst | input drain | `:prof frames` | inspect `YEW_INPUT_BURST_MAX`, early exit, and `EAGAIN` handling |
| First paint is slow only on some terminals | startup | `perf_startup` with the `dumb` profile | find work waiting on capability negotiation |
| First paint is slow everywhere | startup | compare default with `--clean` | config loading or eager file/workspace work |
| Scrolling stutters but typing is fine | `YEW_PH_RENDER` | render byte-count rows | damage widening or suppressed `CSI K` |
| A large paste freezes | input/dispatch | `YEW_PF_BURST_CAP` on the frame | verify burst cap and paste coalescing |
| RSS is several times file size | memory | `perf_mem` checkpoints | massif for heap shape; inspect unreleased snapshots |
| Only `MODULES=""` is slow | build configuration | `make MODULES="" perf` | find a disabled-module shim doing work |
| Local is green, CI is red without a code delta | runner | beginning/end calibration scales | treat drift/refusal as no verdict, not a regression |

## Runner and calibration policy

Only the pinned runners named `perf-x86_64-linux-gnu` and
`perf-arm64-linux` may issue wall-clock verdicts. They use separate,
same-ISA calibration references and separate baselines. A designated run
sets `PERF_GATE=1`, measures calibration before and after the suite, and
enforces both absolute budgets and relative regressions.

Every hosted, shared, developer, unknown, or differently named runner is
advisory. It runs the same metrics so the harness cannot rot, prints
`ADVISORY` and the baseline rows it would write, and exits successfully for
ordinary timing regressions. Finite/nonzero sanity checks and deterministic
rows such as byte counts and frame counts remain hard failures everywhere;
a value above 100 times its budget is a broken harness/feature, not noise.
An unknown runner ID never becomes designated by having a reference file.

`make calib` performs three warmup-discarded, median-of-11 measurements for
pointer-chase latency, branchy scalar work, and memory bandwidth. Calibrated
limits use the equal-weight integer scale in permille. Calibration refuses a
gating verdict when:

- the beginning or ending scale is outside `[500, 3000]`;
- the ending scale differs from the beginning by more than 15 percent;
- runner ID or ISA differs from the designated reference;
- the reference is absent, malformed, incomplete, or still a template.

The 15 percent boundary itself is accepted; only a difference greater than
15 percent refuses. Refusal means **no verdict**, never pass. A single scale
does not model every workload axis, so it is used only on the two pinned
hosts. It does not calibrate syscalls, page faults, fork/exec, or filesystem
I/O: cold start, 100 MB open, and atomic save remain raw designated-host
budgets and are advisory elsewhere.

The checked-in `calib-reference-*.template` files contain no measurements.
They deliberately fail the strict reference parser until an operator on the
corresponding pinned host replaces every `PLACEHOLDER`, records all required
machine metadata, and promotes the result as:

- `calib-reference.txt` for `perf-x86_64-linux-gnu`;
- `calib-reference-arm64.txt` for `perf-arm64-linux`.

Never infer, copy across ISAs, or manufacture a reference vector. Promotion
and both initial baseline commits require evidence from the named hardware.

## External deep dives

Build symbols in a disjoint object tree first:

```sh
make perf-symbols
```

Use the external tools only after the phase table identifies the expensive
phase:

```sh
perf record -g --call-graph=fp -- build-prof/yew --batch tests/perf/sessions/typing.fl f
perf report

perf stat -e cycles,instructions,cache-misses,branch-misses -- \
  build-prof/yew --batch tests/perf/sessions/typing.fl f

valgrind --tool=callgrind build-prof/yew --batch tests/perf/sessions/typing.fl f
callgrind_annotate callgrind.out.*

valgrind --tool=massif --time-unit=B \
  build-prof/yew --batch tests/perf/sessions/typing.fl f

valgrind --tool=dhat build-prof/yew --batch tests/perf/sessions/typing.fl f
```

For an interactive PTY child, spawn it with the performance harness and
attach using the child PID printed by the runner:

```sh
perf record -g --call-graph=fp -p CHILD_PID
```

Do not profile the PTY runner and mistake its cost for yew's. `perf` wall
time includes profiler overhead; hardware counter values are not comparable
between machines. Callgrind gives exact call counts but its timing is not
real. Massif measures heap, not RSS; process RSS comes from the suite's
`getrusage` checkpoints.

## Baselines and rebaselining

Sprint 11's policy remains binding:

1. Designated runners compare the median of three p99 measurements. A
   relative regression is over 10 percent and must occur in two of three
   runs to fail.
2. Advisory runners warn for timing regressions. Deterministic and sanity
   failures remain hard everywhere.
3. An improvement over 20 percent prints `rebaseline me`; it never updates
   files automatically.
4. Unknown runner IDs are advisory and print the row they would commit.
5. Every baseline row has a nonempty `why`; moving a number over 5 percent
   requires replacing an initial placeholder reason with the actual cause.
6. Baseline headers record the calibration vector and `scale_permille`.
   Comparing materially different calibration scales is refused.
7. `make perf-update` is manual. Commit its output alone with a message
   beginning `perf: rebaseline` and list the deltas. The baseline guard
   rejects a commit that touches both `src/` and `tests/perf/baselines/`.

`make perf` and `make perf-huge` capture three complete Sprint 56
observation ledgers before issuing a timing verdict.  The harnesses emit
measurements; `scripts/s56-perf-gate.sh` is the single production boundary
that validates the quick/huge metric set, preserves budget-file order,
applies the median/2-of-3 rule, and performs the runner-derived v2 baseline
comparison.  Hosted runs need no baseline and keep timing advisory, but
`enforcement=all`, frames-greater-than-keys, missing metrics, and the 100x
sanity bound remain hard.  A designated run refuses a missing, incomplete,
zero-calibration, or runner/ISA-mismatched baseline with exit 75.

Baseline files always live under `tests/perf/baselines/` (plural). Never
raise a budget to make a regression green; changing `budgets.txt` changes a
locked product decision and requires explicit review.

## `100m-allnl` decision

Sprint 56 closes the Sprint 11 revisit: the exemption stands and is not
deferred again. `100m-allnl` remains measured and baseline-tracked, but its
end-to-end open time and 1.6-times memory ceiling are informational. The
piece tree's line index necessarily costs eight bytes per one-byte line;
alternatives were already rejected in Sprints 7 and 11. The 1.6-times clean
open quality bar applies to realistic line lengths (`100m-code` and
`100m-utf8`). A relative change in `100m-allnl` must still be visible in the
advisory/baseline output.
